course: biology
lesson: 397
title: Pests: mice in the kitchen, cockroaches, clothes moths, fleas and head lice
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mum opens the cupboard and finds tiny dark droppings like grains of rice and a chewed hole in the cereal packet. A mouse has moved in, because a kitchen is warm, dry and full of food.
explain: We call animals pests when they eat our food, damage our homes or carry germs. They are not evil; they just found an easy place to live. A mouse wants crumbs, a cockroach wants dark damp corners, and a clothes moth's caterpillar wants your wool jumper.
explain: The same animal can be a pest in one place and a helper in another: a house spider eats flies and mosquitoes, so many people let it stay.
explain: The best way to beat pests is to keep them out first: food in tight boxes, crumbs wiped away, gaps sealed, pets treated by a vet. Traps and sprays are for grown-ups and experts, never for children to play with.
fact: House mice leave droppings like grains of rice and gnaw holes in packets when they move in for food and warmth; their teeth never stop growing, so they gnaw almost anything, even wires.
fact: People call pest experts or set traps away from children and pets, and keeping open food in tight boxes stops mice and insects from getting at it.
fact: Cockroaches hide in warm, damp, dark places and come out at night for crumbs; they run from light because darkness keeps them safe from predators.
fact: A cockroach can live for a while without its head, because it breathes through holes along its body, not through its mouth.
fact: Cockroaches survive more radiation than people but are not indestructible: many insects survive radiation better, and cold, heat and hunger still kill them; of thousands of kinds of cockroach, only a few live with people.
fact: Holes in wool jumpers are made by the caterpillars of clothes moths, not by the grown-up moths, which do not eat; the caterpillars eat wool, fur and feathers and prefer dirty clothes in dark wardrobes.
fact: Fleas are tiny wingless insects with six legs, the back pair long and strong for jumping, and flat bodies that slip between hairs; most of a flea problem is eggs and young in the home, not on the pet.
fact: A vet gives pets flea treatment, and people vacuum carpets and wash pet beds.
fact: Head lice are tiny wingless insects that live only on people's heads; they cannot jump or fly, only crawl from hair to hair when heads touch, and they are not a sign of dirty hair.
fact: A fine comb gets head lice out, and clean hair gets lice just as easily as dirty hair.
fact: Fruit flies smell ripe and rotting fruit from far away, lay eggs on it and eat the yeast growing on it; scientists have studied them for over a hundred years to learn how genes work.
fact: Ants follow smell trails left by the first ant that found food, so wiping the trail with soapy water confuses them; ants are a nuisance but not dirty like cockroaches.
fact: Pests eat our food, damage homes or carry germs, but spiders in the house eat flies and mosquitoes; pest experts try to keep animals out first, with sealed gaps and clean kitchens, before using anything dangerous.
exercise: What do mouse droppings look like? | Tiny dark grains like rice | Big round balls like marbles | Little white spots like chalk | Long thin green strings of slime
exercise: Why can mice gnaw through almost anything, even wires? | Their teeth never stop growing | Their front teeth are made of a kind of metal | They have a hundred teeth in each jaw | Their spit melts plastic and wires
exercise: Who should deal with mouse traps? | Grown-ups or pest experts, away from children and pets | Children, because setting them is a fun game for after school | The family dog | Nobody, traps work alone
exercise: Where do cockroaches like to hide? | In warm, damp, dark places | In bright sunny windows | In the freezer | On top of the roof
exercise: Why do cockroaches run from light? | Darkness keeps them safe from predators | Light burns their skin straight away if they stay in it | They are blind in the dark | Light makes them sneeze
exercise: How can a cockroach live for a while without its head? | It breathes through holes along its body | It grows a brand new head at once, like a lizard grows a tail | It keeps a spare head inside | It does not need any air at all
exercise: Would cockroaches survive anything at all? | No, cold, heat and hunger still kill them | Yes, nothing in the whole world can ever kill a cockroach | Yes, they survive even fire | Only cockroaches survive radiation
exercise: What makes the holes in a wool jumper? | The caterpillars of clothes moths | The grown-up moths with their teeth | Mice looking for warmth in the winter | Spiders making webs in the wardrobe
exercise: Which clothes do moth caterpillars like best? | Dirty wool in dark wardrobes | Clean cotton in bright rooms | Plastic raincoats | Shiny new shoes
exercise: Where is most of a flea problem? | As eggs and young in the home, not on the pet | Only on the tip of the pet's tail, where it cannot scratch | Only out in the garden | Only on people's heads
exercise: How many legs does a flea have? | Six | Eight | Four | Ten
exercise: How do fleas get from place to place? | They jump | They fly on wings | They swim through water | They roll like balls
exercise: How do head lice spread? | They crawl from hair to hair when heads touch | They jump from head to head across the room, like fleas | They fly across the classroom | They live in school chairs
exercise: Are head lice a sign of dirty hair? | No, clean hair gets them too | Yes, only dirty hair gets them | Yes, they eat the dirt from hair | Only if you never ever brush it
exercise: Why do fruit flies appear when bananas get brown spots? | They smell the ripening fruit and come to lay eggs | The brown spots on old bananas slowly turn into little flies | They hatch from inside every banana | They like the yellow colour
exercise: How do ants find their way to spilt jam? | They follow a smell trail left by the first ant | They can see the jam all the way from their nest | They hear the jam drip | They follow the sunlight
exercise: Why do many people let house spiders stay? | Spiders eat flies and mosquitoes | Spiders keep the windows nice and clean | Spiders keep the mice out of the house | Spiders bring good weather with them
test: «ew, mice, just leave the cereal open, they'll go away» What is the best advice? | Keep food in tight boxes, so mice find nothing to eat | Leave more food out in the garden so they stay outside the house | Mice hate cereal, so it is fine | Mice only eat cheese
test: Count them: mum finds 3 fleas on the dog. How many legs, and how many wings? | 18 legs and 0 wings | 18 legs and 6 wings | 24 legs and 0 wings | 12 legs and 6 wings
test: Count them: how is a flea different from a tick when you count legs? | A flea has 6 legs and a tick has 8 | A flea has 8 legs and a tick has 6 | Both have 6 legs | Both have 8 legs
test: «cockroaches'd survive the end of the world, mate» What is the truth? | They are tough, but cold, heat and hunger still kill them, and other insects beat them at radiation | True, they cannot be killed by anything at all, not fire, not ice and not hunger, nothing ever | True, they are made of metal | False, they die from light
test: In a cartoon, a giant moth flies into a wardrobe and chews a jumper with big teeth. What is really eating the jumper? | Tiny moth caterpillars, because grown-up moths do not eat | Grown-up moths, which have small sharp teeth hidden under their furry heads | Mice hiding in the sleeves | Nothing, jumpers get holes alone
test: «Jake's got nits, his hair's filthy» What is the best correction? | Lice are not a sign of dirty hair; clean hair gets them too | Right, only dirty hair gets lice, so he should just wash it more | Lice only live on dogs | Lice come from dirty pillows only
test: Three friends put their heads together over one phone. Why might lice spread? | Lice crawl from hair to hair when heads touch | Lice jump across from the phone screen onto anyone holding it | Phones make lice grow | Lice fly between the screens
test: What would happen if you wiped an ant trail with soapy water? | The ants would lose the smell trail and get confused | The ants would follow it even faster, because they like the soap | The ants would turn into flies | Nothing, ants follow sight only
test: The dog was given flea treatment but fleas keep coming back. What has been forgotten? | Vacuuming carpets and washing the pet bed, where eggs and young live | Giving the dog more food | Brushing the dog's teeth every night before bed | Taking the dog for much longer walks in the fields, away from other dogs
test: «there's a spider in the bathroom, kill it!!» Why might you let it stay? | It eats flies and mosquitoes | It keeps the bath nice and clean | It scares all the mice out of the house | It makes the bath water warmer
test: Count them: 7 cockroaches run under the fridge. How many legs? | 42 | 28 | 56 | 49
test: Why would leaving crumbs on the floor at night be an invitation to cockroaches? | Cockroaches come out at night looking for crumbs | Crumbs make cockroaches sleepy, so they stay under the fridge | Cockroaches eat only in daylight | Crumbs scare cockroaches away
exam: Identify the principal reason for which house mice are able to gnaw through a wide variety of materials, including electrical wires. | Their teeth grow continuously throughout their lives | Their teeth are composed of metal | Their saliva contains a substance which dissolves plastic and copper | They possess an exceptional number of teeth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the damage caused to woollen garments by clothes moths? | It is caused by the caterpillars, whereas the adult moths do not feed | It is caused exclusively by the adult moths | It is caused by mice concealed within wardrobes | It is caused by spiders in the construction of webs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the transmission of head lice. | They are transmitted by crawling from hair to hair when heads come into contact | They are transmitted by jumping across considerable distances | They are transmitted by flying between persons | They are transmitted exclusively to persons with unwashed hair
exam: It is given that a flea possesses six legs and a tick possesses eight legs. Ascertain the total number of legs of two fleas and one tick. | Twenty | Fourteen | Twenty-two | Sixteen
